  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/heterotis_niloticus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Heterotis niloticus is a relative of Arapaima gigas, the largest truly freshwater fish, and is highly attractive. With a maximum length of scarcely about one meter it is more suitable for aquarium keeping than its South American relatives.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/heterotis_niloticus_en/",
      "html": "<p>In Africa they originally inhabit different water types south of the Sahara. Origin: Central Africa.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/heterotis_niloticus_en/",
      "html": "<p>This fish differs from all the other bonytongues (Osteoglossidae) in the way it feeds. It feeds mostly on plankton and is the only plankton-feeder of the Osteoglossidae.</p><p>Live red mosquito larvae (bloodworms) have proven to be the ideal food for this species. Even specimens 30-40 cm long eat this food enthusiastically.</p>"
    }
